        body: "Connecticut terminals answer to the Connecticut Airport Authority, which operates Bradley International Airport, and their MEP systems must handle nor'easters and heavy snow, plus coastal corrosion protection while meeting FAA and code requirements. Plumbing and fire protection complete the terminal MEP: restroom cores sized for peak-hour enplanements with risers threaded around structural transfer zones, concessions grease waste and gas distribution, and storm drainage for the enormous roof areas terminals create. Fire protection follows NFPA 13 inside the terminal and NFPA 415 where aircraft fuel at gates — the ramp-drainage and loading-walkway interface most designers miss until permit review. Renovating an operating terminal is a phasing problem as much as an engineering one: temporary HVAC and generator-backed temporary power carry the building through cutovers, with the sequence documented in the construction set so the terminal never closes. Apex Grid writes the phasing, the controls sequences per ASHRAE Guideline 36, and the commissioning requirements into the documents — because terminal performance lives or dies in the controls, not the equipment schedule."
