        body: "Iowa's aviation system supports one of the nation's largest agricultural-aviation sectors, with growing cargo operations at Des Moines and Cedar Rapids. That demand is why Iowa hangar engineering starts with span and door — and with extreme cold and snow design, plus tornado-alley wind criteria. Door selection changes the structure more than any other decision. Bottom-rolling doors — the large-hangar standard — roll on rails demanding near-zero differential settlement; an inch of movement across a 200-foot door jams it, which pushes geotechnical investigation and rail-foundation design harder than the frame in poor soils. Vertical-lift doors hang dozens of tons from the header and need protected headroom; fabric doors shed the lateral bracing a rigid door would share. Span width is the dominant variable: a 60-foot box hangar uses standard rigid frames, while a 200-foot clear-span MRO hangar needs long-span trusses or a space frame with connection forces that demand real engineering. Doubling the span roughly quadruples the frame design effort — member depths, lateral drift, and foundation reactions all escalate. The structural scheme is chosen at programming, because reframing a designed hangar is brutally expensive."
