        body: "Massachusetts transit agencies and airport operators are converging on intermodal facilities, and the buildings must be designed for nor'easters, heavy snow, and coastal corrosion. Security shapes the terminal connection: sterile corridors, screening checkpoints, and access control designed with TSA requirements where the intermodal building ties into the terminal. The security boundary is physical construction — full-height walls, alarmed doors — not a policy. Construction phasing keeps both the transit operation and the airport running, with crane heights through FAA airspace review. The compliance trap is dual: FTA-funded transit work follows Buy America (49 U.S.C. § 5323(j)) while FAA-funded aviation work follows Buy American (49 U.S.C. § 50101) — separate statutes, different tests, and the specs must satisfy the stricter applicable rule per component where funding mixes. Apex Grid tracks both regimes from design development through closeout."
