        body: "The holdroom is where Nevada terminal MEP gets tested: high-bay air, curtain-wall solar gain, and crowds that arrive in waves. The controls layer is where terminal MEP succeeds or fails: sequences that actually implement demand-controlled ventilation, smoke-mode overrides that don't fight normal operation, and integration with the airport operations center for monitoring and alarming. Apex Grid specifies the sequence of operations as a construction document — not a deferred submittal — with the points lists and alarm logic the controls contractor builds to. Plumbing and fire protection complete the terminal MEP: restroom cores sized for peak-hour enplanements with risers threaded around structural transfer zones, concessions grease waste and gas distribution, and storm drainage for the enormous roof areas terminals create. Fire protection follows NFPA 13 inside the terminal and NFPA 415 where aircraft fuel at gates — the ramp-drainage and loading-walkway interface most designers miss until permit review."
